-- title:
--   Lower bound for minimal primes of a monogenic extension
-- statement:
--   Let $R$ and $S$ be commutative rings with $S$ an $R$-algebra, let $f \in R[X]$ be monic, and suppose `IsAdjoinRoot S f` holds, i.e. $S$ is presented as $R[X]/(f)$: there is a surjective ring homomorphism $R[X] \to S$ with kernel the ideal generated by $f$, compatible with the algebra structure, and `hS.root` denotes the image of $X$. Let $n$ be a natural number, and assume that for every minimal prime $\mathfrak p$ of $R$ there is a finite subset $s$ of $\mathrm{Frac}(R/\mathfrak p)$ with $n \le \#s$ all of whose elements are roots of the image of $f$ in $\mathrm{Frac}(R/\mathfrak p)[X]$ (so $f$ has at least $n$ distinct roots in each such fraction field). Assume further that the set of minimal primes of $S$ is finite. Then $n \cdot \#\,\mathrm{Min}(R) \le \#\,\mathrm{Min}(S)$, where the cardinalities are `Set.ncard`, so that the left-hand side is $0$ by convention if $\mathrm{Min}(R)$ is infinite. No reducedness or Noetherian hypothesis is imposed on $R$.
--
--   This is the standard count of irreducible components of a finite monogenic (simple integral) extension: over each generic point of $\operatorname{Spec} R$, every root of $f$ in the residue field of that point contributes a distinct component of $\operatorname{Spec} S$. It is used in the analysis of the rings attached to modular curves at full level, in the two statements bounding the number of minimal primes of the rings arising from Tate points and from $j$-invariants.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_IsAdjoinRoot_mul_ncard_minimalPrimes_le_of_forall_exists_roots.lean

import Mathlib

set_option maxHeartbeats 4000000
